  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nActive weather pattern to continue overnight into Friday.  Another \nMCS moving thru east-southeast thru northern Illinois. This system \nwill likely dive more southeast closer to the instability gradient \ninto portions of eastern Indiana and western Ohio this evening. CAM \nsolutions show a favorable overlap of shear/instability with some of \nthe storms on the leading edge of this cluster having potential to \nbecome severe. Damaging wind gusts will be the primary severe hazard,\nalthough a brief tornado and marginally severe hail are also \npossible. CAM solutions differ on the exact placement and timing \nwith the 00Z HRRR having the placement of the current storms. \nFollowing this solution - the most favorable location and time for \nsevere weather is across eastern Indiana, southwest Ohio, and \nnorthern KY - this evening into the early overnight hours.\n\nAdditional storm development is expected where favorable 8H moisture\nconvergence will setup. Storms oriented from northwest to southeast \nto track into ILN/s FA thru the overnight hours and slowly sag south \nthru the area Friday morning. Environment is primed for heavy rain \nwith PWAT values expected to be near 2 inches. The potential for \nstripes of 2 to 4 inch rainfall and locally 5 plus in repeated storms\novernight. This heavy rain will potentially affect areas already \nhard hit by flooding. \n\nIn the wake of this activity, there is not much of a signal for additional\nstorms the rest of Friday. The flood watch continues tonight into \nFriday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\n\nMid level ridge will build a bit northward over the weekend. This\nwill push the active pattern of storms north of the area. It will \nalso allow for temperatures to warm substantially. There will be the \npotential for heat indices reaching advisory criteria on Saturday and\nSunday. Convection will be much more limited until a cold front \nslides southeast across the region on Sunday night.",
